    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-72059",
      "knowledge_point_id": "cf98ee24-7774-5674-8a1e-63f4f6d225c0",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating a Reciprocal Function",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\int -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}x}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
